module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.KolmogorovSinaiEntropy
public import MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.BernoulliShift
public import Mathlib.Probability.ProductMeasure
public import Mathlib.Probability.UniformOn
public import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.Periodic
public import Mathlib.Analysis.Fourier.AddCircle
public import Mathlib.NumberTheory.Real.Irrational

/-!
# Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy of Bernoulli shifts and the generator theorem: wanted results

The central theorems of Sinai's entropy theory, stated with the definitions of
`MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.KolmogorovSinaiEntropy`:

* `ksEntropy_eq_entropyRel_of_isGenerator`: Sinai's generator theorem (Sinai, Scholarpedia
  *Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy*, Theorem 1; Sarig, Theorem 4.4).
* `ksEntropy_eq_entropyRel_of_isStrongGenerator`: its noninvertible form for strong generators
  (Sarig, Theorem 4.4).
* `ksEntropy_bernoulliShift`: the entropy of the Bernoulli shift with probability vector `p` is
  `-∑ pᵢ log pᵢ` (Sarig, Proposition 4.6).
* `ksEntropy_addCircle_rotation_eq_zero`: irrational rotations have entropy zero (Sarig,
  Proposition 4.7).
* `ornstein_isomorphism`: Bernoulli schemes with equal entropy are isomorphic modulo null sets
  (Ornstein).
* `not_isomorphic_bernoulli_uniform_three_two`: the `(1/3, 1/3, 1/3)` and `(1/2, 1/2)` Bernoulli
  schemes are not isomorphic modulo null sets (Sarig, Proposition 4.6). It follows from
  `ksEntropy_bernoulliShift` and the proved invariance `ksEntropy_eq_of_isIsomorphicMod0`.

The Bernoulli scheme on a finite alphabet `S` with probability vector `p` is the one-sided left
shift `MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.BernoulliShift.shift` on `ℕ → S` with the product measure
`Measure.infinitePi (fun _ ↦ p)` (Sarig, §1.5.3).
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.Dynamics.KolmogorovSinaiWanted

open MeasureTheory
open MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.KolmogorovSinaiEntropy MathlibExt.Dynamics.Ergodic.BernoulliShift
open scoped ENNReal

/-- **Sinai's generator theorem.** Let `T` be an invertible measure-preserving transformation of
a probability space `(α, μ)` and let `ξ = {C₁, …, C_r}` be a finite measurable partition which is
a generator: the σ-algebra generated by all `Tⁿ Cⱼ`, `n ∈ ℤ`, is the whole σ-algebra modulo
`μ`. Then `h_μ(T) = h_μ(T, ξ)` (Ya. G. Sinai, *Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy*, Scholarpedia 4(3):2034,
Definition 2 and Theorem 1, proved in Sinai, Dokl. Akad. Nauk SSSR 124 (1959); O. Sarig,
*Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Definition 4.4 and Theorem 4.4, where finite partitions have
finite entropy). The integer powers are written as forward iterates of `T` and of `T.symm`. -/
theorem_wanted ksEntropy_eq_entropyRel_of_isGenerator {α : Type*} [MeasurableSpace α]
    {μ : Measure α} [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (T : α ≃ᵐ α) (hT : MeasurePreserving T μ μ)
    {r : ℕ} {ξ : Fin r → Set α} (hξ : IsMeasurablePartition μ ξ)
    (hgen : ∀ s : Set α, MeasurableSet s → ∃ t : Set α,
      MeasurableSet[MeasurableSpace.generateFrom
        (Set.range (fun q : ℕ × Fin r => T^[q.1] ⁻¹' ξ q.2) ∪
          Set.range (fun q : ℕ × Fin r => T.symm^[q.1] ⁻¹' ξ q.2))] t ∧ t =ᵐ[μ] s) :
    ksEntropy μ T = entropyRel μ T ξ

/-- **Sinai's generator theorem, noninvertible case.** Let `T` be a measure-preserving
transformation of a probability space `(α, μ)` and let `ξ = {C₁, …, C_r}` be a finite measurable
partition which is a strong generator: the σ-algebra generated by all `T⁻ⁱ Cⱼ`, `i ≥ 0`, is the
whole σ-algebra modulo `μ`. Then `h_μ(T) = h_μ(T, ξ)` (O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic
Theory*, Definition 4.4 and the noninvertible statement of Theorem 4.4). -/
theorem_wanted ksEntropy_eq_entropyRel_of_isStrongGenerator {α : Type*} [MeasurableSpace α]
    {μ : Measure α} [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] {T : α → α} (hT : MeasurePreserving T μ μ)
    {r : ℕ} {ξ : Fin r → Set α} (hξ : IsMeasurablePartition μ ξ)
    (hgen : ∀ s : Set α, MeasurableSet s → ∃ t : Set α,
      MeasurableSet[MeasurableSpace.generateFrom
        (Set.range (fun q : ℕ × Fin r => T^[q.1] ⁻¹' ξ q.2))] t ∧ t =ᵐ[μ] s) :
    ksEntropy μ T = entropyRel μ T ξ

/-- **Entropy of a Bernoulli shift.** The Bernoulli scheme on a finite alphabet `S` with
probability vector `p` has Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy `-∑_{s ∈ S} p(s) log p(s)`
(O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Proposition 4.6, where `log = log₂`; with the
natural logarithm of `partitionEntropy` both sides are rescaled by the same factor). The
computation goes through the generator theorem applied to the partition into 1-cylinders. -/
theorem_wanted ksEntropy_bernoulliShift {S : Type*} [Fintype S] [MeasurableSpace S]
    [MeasurableSingletonClass S] (p : Measure S) [IsProbabilityMeasure p] :
    ksEntropy (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => p) (shift : (ℕ → S) → ℕ → S) =
      ENNReal.ofReal (∑ s, Real.negMulLog (p {s}).toReal)

/-- **Kolmogorov's non-isomorphism of Bernoulli schemes.** The `(1/3, 1/3, 1/3)` and the
`(1/2, 1/2)` Bernoulli schemes are not measure-theoretically isomorphic, even modulo null sets
(`IsIsomorphicMod0`, the notion of O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Definition 1.3),
because their entropies are `log 3 ≠ log 2` (Sarig, Proposition 4.6). Fixed and periodic points
form null sets, so counting them proves nothing here: entropy is needed. -/
theorem_wanted not_isomorphic_bernoulli_uniform_three_two :
    ¬ IsIsomorphicMod0
      (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => ProbabilityTheory.uniformOn (Set.univ : Set (Fin 3)))
      (shift : (ℕ → Fin 3) → ℕ → Fin 3)
      (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℕ => ProbabilityTheory.uniformOn (Set.univ : Set (Fin 2)))
      (shift : (ℕ → Fin 2) → ℕ → Fin 2)

/-- **The irrational rotation has entropy zero.** For irrational `α`, the rotation
`x ↦ x + α` of the circle `ℝ / ℤ` has Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy `0` with respect to the Haar
probability measure `AddCircle.haarAddCircle`, which on `ℝ / ℤ` is the Lebesgue measure `volume`
(O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*, Proposition 4.7). -/
theorem_wanted ksEntropy_addCircle_rotation_eq_zero {α : ℝ} (hα : Irrational α) :
    ksEntropy (AddCircle.haarAddCircle : Measure (AddCircle (1 : ℝ)))
      (fun x => x + (α : AddCircle (1 : ℝ))) = 0

/-- **Ornstein's isomorphism theorem.** Two invertible Bernoulli schemes with the same
Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy are measure-theoretically isomorphic modulo null sets: if the two-sided
shifts on `ℤ → S` and `ℤ → S'` with product measures `p^ℤ` and `q^ℤ` have equal entropy, they
are `IsIsomorphicMod0` (isomorphism as in O. Sarig, *Lecture Notes on Ergodic Theory*,
Definition 1.3). With `ksEntropy_eq_of_isIsomorphicMod0`, entropy is a complete invariant of
Bernoulli schemes ("Two Bernoulli schemes are measure theoretically isomorphic iff their metric
entropies are equal", Sarig, introduction to Chapter 4 and its closing notes; D. Ornstein, 1970).
The isomorphism cannot in general be an everywhere conjugacy: the uniform scheme on 4 letters and
`(1/2, 1/8, 1/8, 1/8, 1/8)` have equal entropy but different numbers of fixed points. The schemes
are two-sided: for one-sided shifts equal entropy does not suffice. -/
theorem_wanted ornstein_isomorphism {S S' : Type*} [Fintype S] [MeasurableSpace S]
    [MeasurableSingletonClass S] [Fintype S'] [MeasurableSpace S'] [MeasurableSingletonClass S']
    (p : Measure S) [IsProbabilityMeasure p] (q : Measure S') [IsProbabilityMeasure q]
    (h : ksEntropy (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℤ => p) (twoSidedShift : (ℤ → S) → ℤ → S) =
      ksEntropy (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℤ => q) (twoSidedShift : (ℤ → S') → ℤ → S')) :
    IsIsomorphicMod0 (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℤ => p) (twoSidedShift : (ℤ → S) → ℤ → S)
      (Measure.infinitePi fun _ : ℤ => q) (twoSidedShift : (ℤ → S') → ℤ → S')

end MathlibExt.Dynamics.KolmogorovSinaiWanted
